    "kind": "lesson",
    "intro": "You already know how TCP handshakes work, how DNS resolves a name, and how HTTP moves bytes between a client and a server. This lesson sits one layer above all of that and asks a different question: given that traffic, who decides what is allowed to happen, and what does that decision actually look like in the logs you read every shift? A firewall is the answer to the first half of that question, and a firewall log line is the answer to the second. Between them sits a surprising amount of nuance that a lot of analysts never get explicitly taught, because 'the firewall blocked it' or 'the firewall allowed it' sounds simple enough that nobody stops to ask what 'allow' actually certifies.\n\nHere is the uncomfortable truth this lesson builds toward: an 'allow' verdict in a firewall log does not mean the traffic was safe. It means the traffic matched a rule that permits it. Those are very different claims. A rule written eighteen months ago to let a vendor's monitoring agent phone home outbound on port 443 will just as happily allow an attacker's command-and-control beacon over that same port, because from the firewall's point of view, both are HTTPS traffic to a permitted destination on a permitted port. The firewall is not judging intent. It is enforcing policy, and policy is only as good as the humans who wrote and maintained it. Understanding this distinction — between 'permitted' and 'benign' — is the single most useful mental shift this lesson can give you, because it is what turns firewall logs from background noise into an active hunting ground.\n\nBy the end of this lesson you will understand the real difference between a stateless packet filter and a stateful firewall that tracks sessions, and why that distinction changes what 'return traffic' even means. You will understand what a next-generation firewall adds on top of a traditional one, and the practical difference between an IDS that watches and an IPS that blocks — including why organizations are often more cautious about IPS than the marketing suggests. You will be able to read a real firewall log line field by field and extract a story from it, especially from the bytes-sent and bytes-received counters that most junior analysts skim past. You will understand egress filtering, the control that most networks get wrong, and why the traffic leaving your network deserves as much scrutiny as the traffic arriving. And you will understand why the source IP address sitting in a firewall log is sometimes not the attacker's machine at all, but a translation artifact you need to unwind before you can act on it.",
