      {
        "heading": "Egress Filtering: the Control Most Networks Skip",
        "content": "Ask most organizations how carefully they control traffic coming *into* their network, and the answer is usually: quite carefully. Ask the same organizations how carefully they control traffic going *out*, and the honest answer is very often: barely at all. This asymmetry has a name — **egress filtering**, or the lack of it — and it is one of the single most consequential, and most commonly neglected, controls in network security, precisely because it sits on the wrong side of the intuitive threat model.\n\nMost security thinking is built around keeping attackers *out*, so ingress rules get written carefully, reviewed, and tightened over years. Almost nobody applies the same discipline to what is allowed to leave, and that gap is exactly what a huge share of real-world command-and-control and data exfiltration relies on.\n\n### Why outbound traffic is left so open\n\nThe practical reason is that outbound rules are perceived as low-risk and high-friction to restrict: internal users need to browse the web, reach cloud services, resolve DNS, receive software updates, and use dozens of third-party SaaS integrations that legitimately need outbound connectivity.\n\nWriting a tight, maintained allow-list for every legitimate outbound destination an organization needs is genuinely hard operational work, and it breaks things visibly and immediately when it is done wrong — a blocked SaaS integration generates a help-desk ticket within minutes, while an *overly permissive* outbound rule generates no complaint at all, because nobody notices traffic that was allowed to leave. This asymmetric feedback loop — restriction is loud and immediate, permissiveness is silent — is exactly why outbound policy tends to drift toward 'allow almost everything' over time unless an organization deliberately fights that drift.\n\n**DNS and HTTPS: the two protocols that are almost always wide open, and why that matters.** Nearly every organization permits outbound DNS queries to resolve domain names, because the internet does not function without it, and nearly every organization permits outbound HTTPS on port 443, because the modern web and virtually every SaaS product depend on it. Attackers know this as well as any network engineer does, which is why an overwhelming share of modern command-and-control frameworks are built to communicate exclusively over HTTPS, blending into the single most common category of outbound traffic any organization generates.\n\n**DNS tunneling** — encoding stolen data or C2 instructions inside the subdomain labels of DNS queries — remains a durable exfiltration technique precisely because DNS is one of the few protocols that is essentially never blocked outbound, even in otherwise well-locked-down environments. Neither protocol is inherently suspicious; the volume of legitimate DNS and HTTPS traffic any organization generates in a day is enormous, which is exactly what gives an attacker's traffic somewhere to hide in plain sight.\n\n### What egress filtering actually looks like when done well\n\nA mature egress posture does not mean blocking DNS or HTTPS outright — that would break the business. It means applying the same rule discipline to outbound traffic that most organizations already apply inbound:\n\n- An explicit allow-list of destinations a given host or segment legitimately needs to reach, with a default-deny posture for everything else.\n- DNS traffic forced through an organization-controlled resolver rather than allowing hosts to query arbitrary external DNS servers directly — this both improves visibility and closes off a common C2 channel.\n- TLS inspection, or at minimum SNI/certificate metadata logging, on outbound HTTPS so that even encrypted traffic's destination and certificate details stay visible for later analysis.\n- Segmentation of which hosts are even allowed to reach the internet directly — a database server, for instance, very rarely has a legitimate reason to make outbound HTTPS calls to arbitrary internet destinations.\n\nClosing off that last path alone removes an enormous amount of attack surface with comparatively little operational cost, because a server-class host's legitimate outbound needs are narrow and well understood.\n\n### What an analyst hunts for in outbound traffic specifically\n\nGiven how wide open egress typically is, the analyst's job becomes finding the anomaly inside a sea of permitted traffic:\n\n- A server-class host that should never initiate outbound connections at all, suddenly doing so.\n- A workstation making outbound HTTPS connections to a destination with a very recently registered domain, or a certificate that does not match the domain being queried.\n- DNS query volume or query length far outside a host's historical baseline, consistent with tunneling.\n- Any outbound connection to infrastructure with no legitimate business relationship to the organization, regardless of how ordinary the port and protocol look on paper."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Segmentation, NAT, and Why the Source IP Lies",
        "content": "Two more concepts round out what an analyst needs to read firewall logs correctly in a real enterprise network, and both of them concern something a beginner naturally assumes to be simple and reliable: that the source IP address in a log line identifies the actual machine that generated the traffic. In a network with segmentation and address translation — which is to say, essentially every real production network — that assumption is only sometimes true, and knowing exactly when it breaks down is a genuinely important investigative skill.\n\n### Network segmentation as a containment strategy\n\nSegmentation divides a network into smaller zones — often implemented with **VLANs** (Virtual Local Area Networks) at the switching layer, enforced with firewall or router policy at the boundaries between them — so that a compromise in one segment does not automatically grant an attacker reach into every other segment.\n\nA well-segmented network might separate user workstations, servers, a database tier, guest wifi, and OT/IoT devices into distinct zones, each with tightly defined rules about what traffic is permitted to cross into the next zone. The security value of this is direct: it converts what would otherwise be a single flat network — where compromising any one device gives an attacker a launching point to reach every other device — into a series of choke points, each one an opportunity for a firewall rule to block lateral movement, and each one a place where a firewall log records the attempt whether it succeeded or not.\n\nWhen you are scoping an incident and asking 'what could this compromised host have reached,' segmentation boundaries and the firewall rules governing them are the actual answer, not just a network diagram someone drew once and never updated.\n\n**NAT and PAT: why the logged source IP is sometimes the gateway, not the culprit.** **Network Address Translation (NAT)** rewrites the source (or destination) IP address of a packet as it crosses a boundary, most commonly so that a large number of internal devices using private IP address ranges can share a small number of public IP addresses to reach the internet. **Port Address Translation (PAT)**, sometimes described as a specific form of NAT, goes further by also rewriting the source port, allowing many internal devices to share a *single* public IP address simultaneously, distinguishing each internal device's traffic purely by which port was assigned to it.\n\nThis is completely ordinary, necessary infrastructure — but it has a direct consequence for log analysis: a firewall or a destination server sitting on the far side of a NAT boundary sees, and logs, only the *translated* address — the NAT device's public IP, not the private IP of the actual internal workstation that generated the traffic. If a hundred workstations all share one outbound NAT gateway, a destination-side log showing malicious-looking traffic from that public IP could, without further correlation, have come from any one of those hundred machines.\n\n### Bridging the gap: how you actually find the real source\n\nThis is precisely the same conceptual trap as the X-Forwarded-For header problem in web application logs, where a reverse proxy or load balancer's IP shows up as the 'source' unless you specifically look at the forwarded-for field to find the client's real address — and the fix requires the same instinct: find the translation record.\n\nOn the firewall itself, the **NAT translation table** (sometimes visible in the same session log as separate 'xlatesrc'/'translated source' fields, sometimes requiring a live lookup on the device) records the mapping between the original internal private IP and port and the translated public IP and port for every active or recently closed session — and matching a destination-side log's source IP and port against that table, at the matching timestamp, resolves the ambiguity.\n\nWhere NAT translation logs are unavailable or already expired, **DHCP lease logs** provide a second path: correlating the timestamp of the suspicious activity against which internal IP address held a given DHCP lease at that moment can at least narrow the internal source down to a specific host, even without a direct NAT mapping. The practical lesson is simple to state and easy to forget under pressure: any time you are working from a destination-side or external-facing log, treat the source IP as provisional until you have confirmed whether a NAT or proxy boundary sits between that log and the actual originating device, and pull the translation or DHCP record before you attribute the activity to a specific machine.",
        "codeExample": "// Segmentation limits blast radius; NAT/PAT can hide the real source\n//\n// SEGMENTATION (VLANs + firewall policy at each boundary)\n//   workstation VLAN -> [firewall rule] -> server VLAN -> [firewall rule] -> DB VLAN\n//   each boundary = a choke point that can BLOCK lateral movement AND\n//   LOG every attempt, whether it succeeded or was denied\n//   scoping question: \"what could this compromised host reach\" = read the\n//   segment boundaries + rules, not an outdated network diagram\n//\n// NAT/PAT — why a destination-side log's source IP can be misleading\n//\n//   100 internal workstations --> [NAT/PAT gateway] --> internet\n//   Destination server sees ONLY the gateway's public IP + assigned port.\n//   \"Malicious traffic from 203.0.113.9\" could be ANY of the 100 workstations\n//   without further correlation.\n//\n// RESOLVING THE REAL SOURCE — same trap as X-Forwarded-For in web logs:\n//   1. Pull the firewall's NAT translation table / xlatesrc-xlatedst fields\n//      for the matching public IP+port at the matching timestamp\n//        translated: 203.0.113.9:41220 <-> internal: 10.20.4.117:51422\n//   2. If NAT records already expired, correlate DHCP lease logs at the\n//      suspicious timestamp to narrow down which internal host held that IP\n//\n// RULE OF THUMB: treat any destination-side source IP as PROVISIONAL until\n// you confirm whether a NAT boundary or proxy sits between you and the\n// actual originating device."
      }
